## FORMULA NODE — verified from Sample 2
===========================================================================
action = "formula". parameters.fields[] holds the field definitions.

⛔ CRITICAL — ONE FIELD PER FORMULA NODE (ORG-VERIFIED 2026-09-07).
A formula node can define EXACTLY ONE field. Putting multiple fields in fields[] fails in the
Recipe Builder with: "The node Formula defines N fields. A node can only define one field."
PROOF: every formula node in the real deployed recipes has exactly 1 field — Sample 2 has 19
formula nodes (all 1-field), Segmentation has 3 (all 1-field). NONE define more than one.

To derive MULTIPLE fields (e.g. Weighted_Pipeline + Opportunity_Age + Opportunity_Status), CHAIN
one formula node PER field: FORMULA0 (Weighted_Pipeline) → FORMULA1 (Opportunity_Age) →
FORMULA2 (Opportunity_Status) → ... each node's sources = [previous node]. Do NOT cram them into
one node's fields[] array. (The multi-field fields[] array is only for computeRelative, NOT formula.)

Each field: { name, label, type, formulaExpression }.

REAL examples (copy the quoting style EXACTLY):
  concat(year(Forecast_Date),'-',month(Forecast_Date),'-W',Rno)
  concat(AccountId,'-',ProductId,'-',year(Forecast_Date), '-', month(Forecast_Date))
  coalesce("ForecastSalesUnitsW-1", null, 0)
  case when "SUM_ForecastSalesUnitsW-1" = 0 then 0 else 1 end
  case when Final_Forecast_SaleUnits < 0 then 'Yes' else 'No' end
  now()

QUOTING RULES (verified — this is the opposite of dashboard SAQL):
  - Field name with special chars (hyphen, etc.): wrap in DOUBLE quotes → "ForecastSalesUnitsW-1"
  - Plain field name (letters/underscore only): bare, no quotes → Forecast_Date, AccountId, Rno
  - STRING literal: SINGLE quotes → 'Yes', 'No', '-'
  - Comparisons: = (single equals), <, >, <=, >=  (NOT ==)

VERIFIED WORKING FUNCTIONS in recipe formula:
  now()                       ← current timestamp (YES it works — DatasetCreatedDate = now())
  year(dateField)             ← extract year
  month(dateField)            ← extract month
  concat(a, b, c, ...)        ← string concatenation
  coalesce(a, b, c)           ← first non-null
  case when <cond> then <v> else <v> end
  Arithmetic: + - * /

Field JSON shape:
```json
{
  "name": "NegativeForecastFlag",
  "label": "Negative Forecast Flag",
  "type": "TEXT",
  "formulaExpression": "case when Final_Forecast_SaleUnits < 0 then 'Yes' else 'No' end"
}
```
type valid values: TEXT, NUMBER, DATETIME, DATE.
For NUMBER add "precision" and "scale". For DATETIME add "format" (e.g. "dd/MM/yyyy").

CHAINED multi-field example (the CORRECT way to derive several fields):
```json
"FORMULA0": { "action": "formula", "parameters": { "expressionType": "SQL",
  "fields": [ { "name": "Weighted_Pipeline", "label": "Weighted Pipeline", "type": "NUMBER",
               "precision": 18, "scale": 2, "formulaExpression": "Amount * Probability / 100" } ] },
  "sources": ["JOIN2"] },
"FORMULA1": { "action": "formula", "parameters": { "expressionType": "SQL",
  "fields": [ { "name": "Opportunity_Status", "label": "Opportunity Status", "type": "TEXT",
               "formulaExpression": "case when IsWon = 'true' then 'Won' when IsClosed = 'true' then 'Lost' else 'Open' end" } ] },
  "sources": ["FORMULA0"] },
"OUTPUT0": { "action": "save", "parameters": { ... }, "sources": ["FORMULA1"] }
```
One field each, chained via sources. NEVER `fields: [f1, f2, f3, f4]` in a single formula node.

NOTE on date differences: the samples do NOT compute datediff in a recipe formula.
They compute date parts (year/month) and build keys, then do time math via
computeRelative (lag/lead) or in the dashboard. If you need "days between two dates",
prefer doing it in the dashboard SAQL step with daysBetween()/date_diff(), OR test
now() arithmetic against a date field in a scratch formula before relying on it.

